WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.758
Negotiation Skills.

2
00:00:02.038 --> 00:00:06.518
Day 24 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Communication phase.

3
00:00:08.268 --> 00:00:09.292
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.572 --> 00:00:20.857
Today's goal: negotiate calmly and fairly, whether it's a deadline at work, the price at a shop, rent with a landlord or terms with a vendor, so both sides walk away satisfied.

5
00:00:22.207 --> 00:00:23.061
Part 1.

6
00:00:23.341 --> 00:00:26.199
Negotiation is a conversation, not a fight.

7
00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:32.932
Good negotiators prepare, listen more than they talk, and look for a deal that works for both sides.

8
00:00:33.212 --> 00:00:37.436
You'll use the same skills later in Salary Negotiation and with clients.

9
00:00:38.786 --> 00:00:39.703
Step 1:

10
00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:43.674
Prepare: your goal, your limit (walk-away point), your alternatives.

11
00:00:43.954 --> 00:00:44.871
Step 2:

12
00:00:45.151 --> 00:00:48.522
Ask and listen: find out what the other side really needs.

13
00:00:48.802 --> 00:00:49.719
Step 3:

14
00:00:49.999 --> 00:00:52.666
Propose: make a clear first offer with a reason.

15
00:00:52.946 --> 00:00:53.906
Step 4:

16
00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:56.618
Trade: give something only when you get something.

17
00:00:56.898 --> 00:00:57.858
Step 5:

18
00:00:58.138 --> 00:01:01.317
Agree and confirm: summarise, then put it in writing.

19
00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:03.541
Part 2.

20
00:01:03.821 --> 00:01:04.824
Key ideas.

21
00:01:06.074 --> 00:01:07.418
Here is a quick table:

22
00:01:07.698 --> 00:01:09.959
Idea, and What it means, and Example.

23
00:01:10.239 --> 00:01:11.349
Walk-away point.

24
00:01:11.629 --> 00:01:12.525
What it means:

25
00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:14.341
The worst deal you'll accept.

26
00:01:14.621 --> 00:01:15.495
Example:

27
00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:18.250
I won't pay more than 30,000 rupeesrent.

28
00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.173
BATNA (best alternative).

29
00:01:20.453 --> 00:01:21.349
What it means:

30
00:01:21.629 --> 00:01:23.250
What you'll do if there's no deal.

31
00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:24.405
Example:

32
00:01:24.685 --> 00:01:28.759
If not this flat, the one in HSR is 31,000 rupees.

33
00:01:29.039 --> 00:01:29.807
Anchor.

34
00:01:30.087 --> 00:01:30.983
What it means:

35
00:01:31.263 --> 00:01:32.970
The first number sets the range.

36
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:34.125
Example:

37
00:01:34.405 --> 00:01:36.517
Make the first offer when you know the market.

38
00:01:36.797 --> 00:01:38.439
Interests versus positions.

39
00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:39.615
What it means:

40
00:01:39.895 --> 00:01:42.562
Why they want something matters more than what they say.

41
00:01:42.842 --> 00:01:43.717
Example:

42
00:01:43.997 --> 00:01:49.991
The PM says Friday because of a client demo, so maybe only the demo part is needed by Friday.

43
00:01:50.271 --> 00:01:51.594
Trade, don't concede.

44
00:01:51.874 --> 00:01:52.770
What it means:

45
00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.650
Never give something for nothing.

46
00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:55.805
Example:

47
00:01:56.085 --> 00:01:59.903
If I take on this extra feature, which one can move to next sprint?

48
00:02:01.253 --> 00:02:02.149
Part 3.

49
00:02:02.429 --> 00:02:04.051
Phrases for every stage.

50
00:02:05.301 --> 00:02:06.645
Here is a quick table:

51
00:02:06.925 --> 00:02:08.247
Stage, and Phrases.

52
00:02:08.527 --> 00:02:09.509
Exploring:

53
00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:15.698
What's most important to you here? / What's driving the deadline? / What flexibility do you have?

54
00:02:15.978 --> 00:02:16.895
Proposing:

55
00:02:17.175 --> 00:02:21.655
Based on reason, I'd suggest... / Would you consider...? / What if we...?

56
00:02:21.935 --> 00:02:22.746
Trading:

57
00:02:23.026 --> 00:02:27.037
If you can... then I can... / I could agree to that, provided that...

58
00:02:27.317 --> 00:02:28.319
Holding firm:

59
00:02:28.599 --> 00:02:33.719
I understand, but that doesn't work for me because... / That's below what I can accept.

60
00:02:33.999 --> 00:02:34.789
Pausing:

61
00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:37.650
Let me think about it and get back to you tomorrow.

62
00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:38.741
Closing:

63
00:02:39.021 --> 00:02:42.818
So, to confirm, we've agreed... / Could you put that in writing?

64
00:02:44.168 --> 00:02:44.808
Tip:

65
00:02:45.088 --> 00:02:46.475
Silence is powerful.

66
00:02:46.755 --> 00:02:48.909
After you make an offer, stop talking.

67
00:02:49.189 --> 00:02:50.875
Let the other person respond.

68
00:02:51.155 --> 00:02:54.483
Nervous people fill the silence and lower their own offer.

69
00:02:55.833 --> 00:02:56.750
Part 4.

70
00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:00.209
Conversation 1: negotiating a deadline at work.

71
00:03:01.459 --> 00:03:03.485
Here is how the conversation goes.

72
00:03:03.765 --> 00:03:06.859
We need the export feature by Friday for the client demo.

73
00:03:07.139 --> 00:03:08.995
I want to help with the demo.

74
00:03:09.275 --> 00:03:11.813
What exactly will the client see on Friday?

75
00:03:12.093 --> 00:03:14.675
They want to see a report downloaded as Excel.

76
00:03:14.955 --> 00:03:15.893
Okay.

77
00:03:16.173 --> 00:03:20.547
The full feature, with PDF, scheduling and email, needs two weeks.

78
00:03:21.897 --> 00:03:23.497
The conversation continues.

79
00:03:23.777 --> 00:03:25.291
Two weeks is too late.

80
00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:32.099
What if we deliver Excel export only by Friday, for the demo, and the rest by the 20th?

81
00:03:32.379 --> 00:03:34.214
Can you include PDF too?

82
00:03:34.494 --> 00:03:39.251
If I add PDF, I'll need to move the search bug fix to next week.

83
00:03:39.531 --> 00:03:40.961
Which matters more?

84
00:03:42.311 --> 00:03:43.911
The conversation continues.

85
00:03:44.191 --> 00:03:45.663
The demo matters more.

86
00:03:45.943 --> 00:03:49.313
Excel and PDF by Friday, the bug fix next week.

87
00:03:49.593 --> 00:03:50.340
Great.

88
00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:51.964
So, to confirm:

89
00:03:52.244 --> 00:03:59.860
Excel and PDF export by Friday, scheduling and email by the 20th, and the search fix next week.

90
00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.847
I'll put this in the ticket.

91
00:04:03.197 --> 00:04:04.157
Part 5.

92
00:04:04.437 --> 00:04:07.167
Conversation 2: negotiating with a vendor.

93
00:04:08.417 --> 00:04:10.444
Here is how the conversation goes.

94
00:04:10.724 --> 00:04:15.524
The annual maintenance contract for your office ACs is 1,20,000 rupees.

95
00:04:15.804 --> 00:04:16.615
Thanks.

96
00:04:16.895 --> 00:04:18.367
We have 20 units.

97
00:04:18.647 --> 00:04:23.169
Another vendor quoted 95,000 rupeesfor the same service.

98
00:04:23.449 --> 00:04:27.204
Our response time is faster: four hours instead of twenty-four.

99
00:04:27.484 --> 00:04:28.785
That's valuable.

100
00:04:29.065 --> 00:04:33.887
Could you match 1,00,000 rupeesif we sign for two years?

101
00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:37.345
For two years, I can do 1,05,000 rupees.

102
00:04:38.695 --> 00:04:40.295
The conversation continues.

103
00:04:40.575 --> 00:04:47.103
If you include two free gas refills each year, we can agree on 1,05,000 rupees.

104
00:04:47.383 --> 00:04:49.751
Okay, two free gas refills per year.

105
00:04:50.031 --> 00:04:50.970
Deal.

106
00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:51.997
Great.

107
00:04:52.277 --> 00:04:57.439
Please send the contract with the four-hour response time and the refills written in.

108
00:04:58.789 --> 00:04:59.728
Part 6.

109
00:05:00.008 --> 00:05:01.587
Everyday negotiations.

110
00:05:02.837 --> 00:05:03.647
Market:

111
00:05:03.927 --> 00:05:04.973
That's a bit high.

112
00:05:05.253 --> 00:05:07.834
Could you do 400 rupeesif I take two?

113
00:05:08.114 --> 00:05:09.031
Landlord:

114
00:05:09.311 --> 00:05:12.362
Other flats nearby ask for three months' deposit.

115
00:05:12.642 --> 00:05:13.943
Would you accept three?

116
00:05:14.223 --> 00:05:15.141
Car dealer:

117
00:05:15.421 --> 00:05:19.687
If I book today, can you include the accessories kit and free first service?

118
00:05:19.967 --> 00:05:20.693
Team:

119
00:05:20.973 --> 00:05:24.813
I'll take the on-call on Diwali week if you cover my New Year week.

120
00:05:26.163 --> 00:05:27.101
Part 7.

121
00:05:27.381 --> 00:05:28.683
Mistakes to avoid.

122
00:05:29.933 --> 00:05:30.786
Avoid:

123
00:05:31.066 --> 00:05:33.093
Accepting the first offer immediately.

124
00:05:33.373 --> 00:05:34.226
Avoid:

125
00:05:34.506 --> 00:05:38.581
Giving a reason the other side can easily reject (I can't afford it).

126
00:05:38.861 --> 00:05:39.714
Avoid:

127
00:05:39.994 --> 00:05:42.725
Getting angry or making threats you won't carry out.

128
00:05:43.005 --> 00:05:43.858
Avoid:

129
00:05:44.138 --> 00:05:46.314
Forgetting to confirm the deal in writing.

130
00:05:47.664 --> 00:05:48.539
Part 8.

131
00:05:48.819 --> 00:05:49.736
Practice.

132
00:05:50.986 --> 00:05:57.749
Plan a negotiation you'll have this month: your goal, your walk-away point, your alternative, and one thing you could trade.

133
00:05:59.099 --> 00:05:59.995
Part 9.

134
00:06:00.275 --> 00:06:01.320
Speak today.

135
00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:06.837
Ask one What's most important to you here? question in a real discussion.

136
00:06:07.117 --> 00:06:09.549
Use the If you... then I... pattern once.

137
00:06:10.899 --> 00:06:12.435
That's the end of this lesson.

138
00:06:12.715 --> 00:06:17.365
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

139
00:06:17.645 --> 00:06:23.768
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

